        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This study provides specific LC-MS/MS analytical parameters for quantifying adenosine and inosine in murine tumor tissues, including column type (ZORBAX RRHD Eclipse Plus C18), mobile phase gradients (water/acetonitrile with 0.1% FA), flow rate (300 µL min⁻¹), and MRM transitions (inosine 269.1/136.8, adenosine 268.1/135.9). It also describes a solid-phase microextraction (SPME) technique for measuring free metabolite concentrations in the tumor microenvironment using HLB-PAN fibers and kinetic calibration. A baseline adenosine concentration of 0.04 µM is reported for control mouse tumors.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study details the biological context of inosine generation via adenosine deaminase (ADA) expressed by engineered probiotics in hypoxic tumor regions. It reports immune outcomes associated with inosine accumulation, such as increased CD8+ T cell infiltration and M1 macrophage polarization in mouse colorectal cancer models. The text notes that inosine can serve as an alternative carbon source for T cells under glucose-depleted conditions and describes the use of lamivudine as an internal standard for quantification.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied material does not provide standardized protocols for human serum or tumor tissue, as all experiments were conducted in mouse models (MC38 subcutaneous and orthotopic CRC). There is no information on short-chain fatty acid (SCFA) quantification methods or parameters. The study does not offer cancer-specific reference ranges for human patients or healthy controls, nor does it cite consensus statements or guidelines for clinical metabolite quantification. The analytical method described is specific to this research context rather than a validated clinical standard.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Search for peer-reviewed methodological papers or consensus guidelines specifically validating LC-MS/MS or GC-MS protocols for inosine and SCFA quantification in human clinical samples (serum, plasma, tumor biopsy). Look for studies that establish reference concentration ranges for these metabolites in cancer patients versus healthy controls. Investigate whether any oncology or metabolomics societies have published standard operating procedures for these specific analytes.\"\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to analyze a captured paper text against a specific gap question about metabolite quantification protocols for inosine and short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) in human serum or tumor tissue.\n\n**Gap Question:** \"What standardized analytical protocols (LC-MS/MS or GC-MS parameters, sample processing standards) exist for quantifying absolute physiological concentrations of inosine and short-chain fatty acids in human serum or tumor tissue, and are there cancer-specific reference ranges or consensus statements?\"\n\n**Success Criteria:**\n1. Identification of at least one peer-reviewed methodological paper or consensus guideline specifying:\n    (1) analytical platform parameters for inosine or SCFA quantification in human serum/tumor samples,\n    (2) sample processing standards (extraction, storage), and\n    (3) reference concentration ranges in cancer patients or healthy controls.\n\n**Material Reading Text:**\nThe provided text is a full research article about engineered *E. coli* Nissle 1917 (EcN) expressing adenosine deaminase (ADA) to convert adenosine to inosine in the tumor microenvironment (TME).\n- It describes in vitro and in vivo (mouse) experiments.\n- It mentions quantification of adenosine and inosine in murine tumor tissues using HPLC-MS/MS (UHPLC coupled to Triple Quad tandem mass spectrometer).\n- It provides specific instrument parameters for this quantification (column, mobile phase, flow rate, MRM transitions, etc.).\n- It mentions a concentration of adenosine in TME was 0.04 µM in the control group (mouse).\n- It does *not* mention Short-Chain Fatty Acids (SCFAs).\n- It does *not* use human serum or human tumor tissue (it uses mouse models: MC38 subcutaneous and orthotopic CRC).\n- It does *not* provide reference ranges for cancer patients or healthy controls (it provides mouse tumor concentrations).\n- It does *not* claim to be a standardized protocol or consensus guideline for human clinical use.\n\n**Review Planning B Content:**\n- Focuses on the therapeutic mechanism (probiotic, adenosine metabolism, TME remodeling).\n- Mentions the SPME technique for adenosine/inosine quantification in murine tumors.\n- Does not mention SCFAs.\n- Does not mention human reference ranges.\n\n**Task:**\n1. Explain what the study studied and found.\n2. Explain how that material may help with the requested gap.\n3. Assign status (fulfilled, partial, unmet, unjudgeable).\n4. Assign material_usefulness (direct, supporting, none).\n5. Fill useful_material, auxiliary_material, remaining_gap, outline_action.\n6. Follow JSON format.\n7. Adhere to constraints (do not call gap fulfilled if criteria open, keep findings attached to study, etc.).\n\n**Analysis:**\n- **Gap Requirement:** Standardized protocols for *human* serum/tumor, *inosine* AND *SCFAs*, reference ranges in *cancer patients/healthy controls*.\n- **Paper Content:** Protocols for *mouse* tumor tissue, *inosine* (and adenosine), *no SCFAs*, *no human reference ranges*.\n- **Match:**\n    - Inosine quantification method: Yes (LC-MS/MS parameters provided for mouse tissue).\n    - SCFA quantification method: No.\n    - Human samples: No (Mouse).\n    - Reference ranges for humans: No.\n    - Standardized/Consensus: No (This is a specific research method, not a consensus guideline).\n\n**Status:** Unmet. The paper does not meet the success criteria regarding human samples, SCFAs, or reference ranges/consensus. It provides a method for inosine in mouse tumors, which is related but not fulfilling the gap.\n\n**Material Usefulness:** Supporting. It provides technical details on LC-MS/MS quantification of inosine in tumor tissue (albeit mouse), which could inform protocol development, but it does not directly answer the gap question about human standards/ranges.\n\n**Useful Material:** The specific LC-MS/MS parameters for inosine/adenosine quantification in tumor tissue (column, mobile phase, MRM transitions). The mention of SPME for free adenosine/inosine. The measured concentration in mouse control (0.04 µM adenosine).\n\n**Auxiliary Material:** The mechanism of adenosine-to-inosine conversion, the therapeutic context (engineered probiotics), the immune effects (T cell, macrophage). These are relevant to the *topic* (metabolite quantification in cancer context) but not the *gap* (standardized human protocols).\n\n**Remaining Gap:** No human protocols, no SCFA protocols, no reference ranges for humans, no consensus guidelines found in this text.\n\n**Outline Action:** Need to search for methodological papers specifically targeting human serum/tumor, SCFA quantification methods, and consensus statements/reference ranges.\n\n**Constraints Check:**\n- Do not call gap fulfilled. (Status: unmet)\n- Keep findings attached to the study. (Refer to \"this study\", \"mouse models\", etc.)\n- Do not infer study design from silence. (Explicitly state it's mouse, not human).\n- Do not produce quotation ledger, etc.\n- JSON format.\n\n**Drafting the JSON:**\n- status: \"unmet\"\n- material_usefulness: \"supporting\"\n- useful_material: Details on LC-MS/MS parameters for inosine/adenosine in tumor tissue (mouse), SPME technique description.\n- auxiliary_material: Therapeutic context, immune modulation mechanisms, mouse model concentrations.\n- remaining_gap: Lack of human sample protocols, SCFA methods, human reference ranges, consensus guidelines.\n- outline_action: Search for human clinical method validation papers, SCFA specific quantification guidelines, and consensus statements on metabolite reference ranges in oncology.\n\n**Refining the description:**\n- Ensure I distinguish between what the paper did (mouse tumor inosine) and what the gap wants (human serum/tumor inosine + SCFA).\n- Highlight that the analytical parameters are specific to this study's context (mouse tumor homogenate/SPME), not a validated human clinical protocol.\n- Note the absence of SCFA data entirely.\n\n**Final Check:**\n- Did I mention SCFAs? The gap asks for Inosine AND SCFAs. The paper has Inosine but NO SCFAs. This is a major miss for the gap.\n- Did I mention Human? The gap asks for Human. The paper is Mouse. This is a major miss.\n- Did I mention Reference Ranges? The gap asks for Cancer/Healthy ranges. The paper gives one mouse control value (0.04 uM adenosine), not a range for humans.\n- Status must be \"unmet\".\n- Material usefulness is \"supporting\" because the analytical method details *could* be adapted, but they don't fulfill the requirement.\n\nLet's construct the JSON.",
